//! name: fuzz_empty_parens_binding
//! rule: 07.Grammar
//! expect: parse-error
//! detail: found by tools/fuzz. `binding = ident | "_" | "(" binding
//!   { "," binding } [ "," ] ")"` requires at least ONE `binding` inside
//!   the parens: unlike `tuple_or_paren`'s value form (`"(" [ expr {","
//!   expr} [","] ] ")"`, whose element list is wrapped in `[ ]` and so
//!   allows zero, spelling the unit value `()`), the first `binding` here
//!   is NOT inside an optional bracket. `tools/ref/fors_parse.py`
//!   correctly enforces this (its `clist(s.binding, ")", 1)` passes a
//!   minimum count of 1 and reports "expected at least 1 item" on `()`);
//!   the Rust parser currently accepts `let () = 0;` as though `()` were
//!   the unit value reused as a binding, which the grammar does not
//!   provide for. Also reproduces via `for () in xs { }` and any other
//!   `binding` slot.

fn f() {
    let () = 0;
}
